In this lesson, we'll look at solving linear equations algebraically, which just means finding the value of the variable that makes the equation true. The core idea is to use inverse operations to isolate the variable on its own. Think of it like unwrapping a present, working from the outside in. Here's the recipe in two steps. First, undo any addition or subtraction to move the constant away from the variable. Second, undo any multiplication or division so the variable stands alone. Once you've got an answer, always substitute it back into the original equation to check both sides match. If they do, you've nailed it.

Now, what if the variable appears on both sides? No stress. Just subtract the smaller variable term from both sides first to get everything onto one side, then carry on as normal. And if there are brackets, expand them first using the distributive law before you start undoing operations. The substitution check is your best friend here too, because it catches silly mistakes before they cost you marks. Now let's see it in action.